+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,133 @@
+# arXiv:2308.11427 — PBW normal forms with hidden coefficient-sum symmetry
+
+This is a **Track B** generator. Its native domain is **algebra**, its object regime is **finite-field exact**, its computational core is **polynomial identity**, and its certificate is a **polynomial**. The solver receives a permutation-idempotent Yang--Baxter algebra and a factored noncommutative polynomial, so `domain_essentiality="native"`; there is no reduction. The intended intuition is an **invariant**: PBW reduction retains only each nonfinal factor's coefficient sum, while independently permuted coefficient rows hide that those sums repeat.
+
+External status: the module and every local G1--G9(c) gate are complete, but this result is **not yet externally hardened or shippable**. The required `harden.py` run made four redraw attempts and every call received HTTP 403 because the configured OpenRouter key had zero limit remaining. The retained `llm_loop_transcript.jsonl` contains those errors; they are not model failures. No hinted/placebo transcript can honestly be produced until that credential is replenished.
+
+## The problem
+
+The source is Gateva-Ivanova and Majid, [*Quadratic algebras associated to permutation idempotent solutions of the YBE*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.11427). Section 2.2 defines the Yang--Baxter algebra. Proposition 3.1 gives the exact maps (r_f(x,y)=(f(y),y)). Theorem 3.4 supplies the reduced PBW relations (x_i x_j=x_0x_j), after choosing the distinguished first generator, and Corollary 3.7 gives the normal form of every word.
+
+An instance asks for the six nonzero coefficients of the PBW normal form of a sum of three factored polynomials over `GF(p)`. Verification scans the factors, adds and multiplies coefficients modulo `p`, and compares the unique sparse normal form exactly. The answer is known by construction: in each branch all dense rows are permutations of one coefficient multiset with a sampled nonzero sum; those certified sums compose multiplicatively with a two-term terminal factor.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+The prior-triage proposal—construct or recover (r_f)—does not clear hardness: Proposition 3.1 writes it down directly, and `f` can be read from any row. This family instead uses the paper's PBW normal-form theorem. The reference algorithm sums every displayed row and costs `O(branches*factors*n)`; at the configured hard preset it performs exactly 18,531 field operations per instance and averaged 0.004818 seconds locally (8/8 solved). Thus Track A would be false.
+
+The compact route notices the repeated coefficient multiset, sums only one row in each branch, computes three modular powers, and scales six terminal coefficients. At hard this is at most 237 exact operations. That is short enough once the invariant is found but is not the mechanical 18,531-operation scan a no-tool model can execute in context.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+This is the complete `demo` instance for seed 0:
+
+```text
+PBW normal form in a permutation-idempotent Yang--Baxter algebra
+
+Work over the prime field GF(11); reduce every coefficient modulo 11.
+Let A be the associative noncommutative algebra on generators x_0,...,x_6
+with relations x_i x_j = x_6 x_j for every 0 <= i,j < 7.
+Thus the degree-4 PBW basis is x_6^3 x_j (0 <= j < 7).
+A coefficient row [c_0,...,c_(n-1)] denotes the linear form sum_i c_i x_i.
+Multiplication is in the printed left-to-right order; the three branch products are added.
+All indices are 0-based, all intervals are inclusive, and repeated generators in a linear form are combined modulo p.
+
+The polynomial F is the sum of these three products. Each D-row is one dense linear factor; T is the final sparse factor.
+BRANCH 0
+D0: [8,9,4,3,7,5,5]
+D1: [3,7,5,4,8,9,5]
+D2: [9,5,5,3,7,4,8]
+T: [(8,x_2),(9,x_4)]
+BRANCH 1
+D0: [1,6,10,3,0,8,0]
+D1: [0,8,0,1,6,10,3]
+D2: [0,10,3,0,1,6,8]
+T: [(2,x_0),(9,x_1)]
+BRANCH 2
+D0: [8,10,9,9,8,4,3]
+D1: [8,3,9,4,8,9,10]
+D2: [8,4,3,10,9,9,8]
+T: [(9,x_3),(4,x_5)]
+
+Find the unique PBW normal form F = sum_j a_j x_6^3 x_j.
+The three terminal supports are [[2,4],[0,1],[3,5]]; they are disjoint.
+The answer is guaranteed to have exactly two nonzero terms on each terminal support.
+Within each support, its two coefficients must be one common nonzero GF(p) scalar multiple of the displayed T coefficients.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as compact JSON
+[[coefficient,generator],...] with exactly six terms, ordered by strictly increasing generator.
+Use canonical coefficients 1,...,10; do not include zero terms.
+Example of the syntax: <answer>[[1,0],[2,1],[3,2],[4,3],[5,4],[6,5]]</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The answer is `[[3,0],[8,1],[4,2],[7,3],[10,4],[8,5]]`; `verify(inst, inst["answer"])` returns `(True, "ok")`. Changing its first coefficient to 4 returns `(False, "coefficient mismatch at generator 0")`. A person can solve this demo on paper: it needs three seven-entry sums and three small modular cubes after spotting the row-permutation symmetry.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | generators `n` | factors/branch | field | displayed dense coefficients | structured space |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| demo | 7 | 4 | GF(11) | 63 | 1,000 |
+| easy | 16 | 20 | GF(101) | 912 | 1,000,000 |
+| medium | 32 | 48 | GF(503) | 4,512 | 126,506,008 |
+| hard (configured shipping) | 64 | 96 | GF(1009) | 18,240 | 1,024,192,512 |
+
+No preset has an oracle verdict because the pool was unreachable. `escalate()` first increases the factor haystack while retaining the six-term answer, then increases `n`; it reports `cap_bound` only when the compact route itself would exceed 300 operations.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | result | measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 16/16 planted witnesses; 16/16 JSON round trips |
+| G2 | pass | drop/swap/duplicate/empty/out-of-range all rejected with 5 distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | realistic prose/fenced answer recovered; garbage returns `None` |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structured guesses; exact density (1/1008^3=9.764\times10^{-10}) |
+| G5 | pass | hard reference scan 8/8, 18,531 ops/instance; demo brute-force count 1 |
+| G6 | pass | five attacks below all 0/8; reference scan intentionally 8/8 |
+| G7 | pass | `n=128` builds/verifies; dense data doubles; answer remains six terms |
+| G8 | pass | 60 invariance and 60 carried-witness checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 55 chars, about 14 tokens, 12 atoms; intended route 237 operations |
+
+G6 attacks were `outlier_mode_power`, `greedy_leading_product`, `copy_terminal_factors`, `single_shared_scalar_ansatz`, and `random_restart_256`. Each failed on all eight shipping seeds.
+
+## Oracle loop
+
+| preset | model | seed | outcome | reason |
+|---|---|---:|---|---|
+| easy | x-ai/grok-4.6 | 236233393 | error | HTTP 403 key limit exceeded |
+| easy | openai/gpt-5.6-terra | 1820931032 | error | HTTP 403 key limit exceeded |
+| easy | openai/gpt-5.6-terra | 1490839789 | error | HTTP 403 key limit exceeded |
+| easy | x-ai/grok-4.6 | 589450649 | error | HTTP 403 key limit exceeded |
+
+The harness then exited “oracle pool is unreachable”; no `solved` or `failed` outcome was counted and `.meta.json` contains no `harden_verdict`.
+
+## G9 arms
+
+| arm | solved/attempts | conclusion |
+|---|---:|---|
+| bare | 0/0 | blocked before a valid oracle response |
+| structural hint | 0/0 | not run |
+| placebo hint | 0/0 | not run |
+
+`hinted - placebo` is undefined pending valid attempts (stored as `0.0` in the schema placeholder). The structural hint names only the coefficient-multiset invariant. No claim about whether it helps an oracle is made.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import gen_2308_11427 as g
+
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=17, **g.DIFFICULTY["hard"])
+statement = g.render(inst)
+candidate = g.parse_answer("<answer>" + __import__("json").dumps(inst["answer"]) + "</answer>")
+assert g.verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+After OpenRouter capacity is restored, rerun `python3 ../../scripts/harden.py gen_2308_11427.py`, then run the structural and placebo copies in separate scratch directories as specified by the task. From the repository root, emission is `bash scripts/emit.sh 2308.11427` after a genuine hardened verdict exists.
+
+## Caveats
+
+- This is deliberately not a complexity-theoretic hardness claim. Any script can solve hard in milliseconds; Track B measures discovery of the compression without tools.
+- The 0/200,000 estimate samples the strongest freely deducible bounded prior used here—three independent nonzero branch scalars. It measures guessability, not resistance to algebraic analysis.
+- A multiset sort/hash attack would expose the invariant and solve every instance. That is the intended tool-assisted route, not a failing attack; the reference scan already establishes that efficient computation exists.
+- The hard renderer is about 75,000 characters. Although the answer and post-insight arithmetic meet G9(c), failure may partly reflect attention over a large coefficient table; the missing oracle run is therefore especially important.
+- `canonical_key` intentionally forgets cross-row column alignment after recording every row multiset and terminal coefficient multiset. This is invariant under all tested semantic symmetries and separates 20/20 unrelated seeds, but it can over-collapse adversarial non-generator inputs sharing those invariants.
+- No SAT/SMT, Gröbner-basis package, or external CAS attack was run. Those would be unnecessary overkill for this PBW presentation and would succeed, consistently with Track B.
