NOTES = """\
Section 2.2 defines the Yang--Baxter algebra from a finite quadratic set.
Proposition 3.1 fixes the exact permutation-idempotent definition
r_f(x,y)=(f(y),y) and also shows why the prior-triage task of recovering or
checking r_f is too easy: f is simply one row of r_f.  Theorem 3.4 gives the
reduced Groebner basis x_i x_j - x_0 x_j (using this module's 0-based
indices), and Corollary 3.7 says every word y_1...y_(d-1)x_j has normal form
x_0^(d-1)x_j.  These two results license the native normal-form task here.

Certificate construction is a composition of identities.  For each summand,
the generator first samples a nonzero target sum, samples a coefficient vector
uniformly subject to that sum, hides the conditioning coordinate by shuffling,
and uses independent permutations of that same multiset as all dense factors.
The planted normal-form scalar is therefore the known target sum to the
(factors-1)-st power.  It is multiplied into a disjoint two-term terminal
factor.  No generated instance is searched.

What makes it easy, and why this is Track B: Theorem 3.4 makes an ordinary
normal-form algorithm linear in all displayed coefficients.  The family is
not Track A.  At the hard preset that reference algorithm performs 18,531
exact field operations.  The compact route notices that coefficient sum is
