README.md:9:The source is Gateva-Ivanova and Majid, [*Quadratic algebras associated to permutation idempotent solutions of the YBE*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.11427). Section 2.2 defines the Yang--Baxter algebra. Proposition 3.1 gives the exact maps (r_f(x,y)=(f(y),y)). Theorem 3.4 supplies the reduced PBW relations (x_i x_j=x_0x_j), after choosing the distinguished first generator, and Corollary 3.7 gives the normal form of every word.
README.md:15:The prior-triage proposal—construct or recover (r_f)—does not clear hardness: Proposition 3.1 writes it down directly, and `f` can be read from any row. This family instead uses the paper's PBW normal-form theorem. The reference algorithm sums every displayed row and costs `O(branches*factors*n)`; at the configured hard preset it performs exactly 18,531 field operations per instance and averaged 0.004818 seconds locally (8/8 solved). Thus Track A would be false.
README.md:17:The compact route notices the repeated coefficient multiset, sums only one row in each branch, computes three modular powers, and scales six terminal coefficients. At hard this is at most 237 exact operations. That is short enough once the invariant is found but is not the mechanical 18,531-operation scan a no-tool model can execute in context.
README.md:74:No preset has an oracle verdict because the pool was unreachable. `escalate()` first increases the factor haystack while retaining the six-term answer, then increases `n`; it reports `cap_bound` only when the compact route itself would exceed 300 operations.
