diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 92cff0259fbf90769b52295bc66437f5b39ad31b..eb8ca065291bfcea40d31763dd623b3601b89d26
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -6,13 +6,13 @@
 
 ## The problem
 
-The source is Gateva-Ivanova and Majid, [*Quadratic algebras associated to permutation idempotent solutions of the YBE*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.11427). Section 2.2 defines the Yang--Baxter algebra. Proposition 3.1 gives the exact maps (r_f(x,y)=(f(y),y)). Theorem 3.4 supplies the reduced PBW relations (x_i x_j=x_0x_j), after choosing the distinguished first generator, and Corollary 3.7 gives the normal form of every word.
+The source is Gateva-Ivanova and Majid, [*Quadratic algebras associated to permutation idempotent solutions of the YBE*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.11427). Section 2.2 defines the Yang--Baxter algebra. Proposition 3.1 gives the exact maps (r_f(x,y)=(f(y),y)). Theorem 3.4 supplies the reduced PBW relations (x_i x_j=x_1x_j), after choosing the distinguished first generator, and Corollary 3.6 gives the normal form of every word. The module uses 0-based labels and randomly relabels that distinguished generator.
 
 An instance asks for the six nonzero coefficients of the PBW normal form of a sum of three factored polynomials over `GF(p)`. Verification scans the factors, adds and multiplies coefficients modulo `p`, and compares the unique sparse normal form exactly. The answer is known by construction: in each branch all dense rows are permutations of one coefficient multiset with a sampled nonzero sum; those certified sums compose multiplicatively with a two-term terminal factor.
 
 ## Why Track B
 
-The prior-triage proposal—construct or recover (r_f)—does not clear hardness: Proposition 3.1 writes it down directly, and `f` can be read from any row. This family instead uses the paper's PBW normal-form theorem. The reference algorithm sums every displayed row and costs `O(branches*factors*n)`; at the configured hard preset it performs exactly 18,531 field operations per instance and averaged 0.004818 seconds locally (8/8 solved). Thus Track A would be false.
