\begin{cor}
\label{thm:segremap}
As above, let  $(X, r_f)$, $(Y, r_{\varphi})$  be finite permutation idempotent  solutions on disjoint sets $X = \{x_1, \cdots,
x_m\}$, $Y = \{y_1, \cdots, y_n\}$ and $A=\cA(\k,X,r_f)$, $B=\cA(\k,Y,r_\varphi)$. Let $(Z,r_\Phi)$  be the solution on $Z =
\{z_{11}, \cdots, z_{mn}\}$ in Definition~\ref{def:Z} and  $A_Z= \cA(\k ,  Z, r_\Phi)$.  The image of the Segre map
$s_{m, n} : A_Z \longrightarrow A\tens_\k B$ is the Segre product $A\circ B$. Moreover, the Segre map is an isomorphism of graded
algebras $s_{m, n} : A_Z \longrightarrow A\circ B$.
\end{cor}



